t(39, 1630, """“I would not decide a matter” .. showing her weakness before the male advisers and making them feel that their word is respected and their opinion has weight.
"She said: Indeed kings" .. it is she who said it, and not the chiefs who said "indeed kings"
As for the chiefs, who were men, they said the opposite of her words; they said what expressed the psychology of man and his nature, entirely different from the woman
Then she confirmed once again the domination of the psychological side affecting a woman, by asserting, without trying a war, that the fate of her kingdom would be ruin and destruction, through the words: "and thus do they do", meaning the matter will happen, will happen, and we will be lost, and our kingdom will be occupied by the Prophet Solomon and his army.
And as a result of the domination of the woman's model of creation and her view of matters, the result very suited to the mind and psychology of the woman is: I will send a gift
Allah's prophet Solomon considered the gift an insult to the call of monotheism that occupied him; when he sent the first letter his concern was monotheism and calling to Allah the Exalted, but Bilqis understood the matter from another angle, which is the angle of political accommodations that suit the mind, make-up and soul of a woman and the nature of her creation. But the gift did not succeed, because Allah's prophet Solomon was in one valley and Queen Bilqis in another valley. When Allah's prophet Solomon understood the matter as an insult, he sent the second, harsh-toned letter, which carried the clear threat and promise of punishment with a plain warning.""")

